body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

Step 10.3 says:
Set [this](https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#this)'s [event](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-navigationprecommitcontroller-event)'s [target](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#event-target)'s [ongoing API method tracker](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#ongoing-api-method-tracker)'s [serialized state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#navigation-api-method-tracker-state) to serializedState.

Problem with this is, for some scenarios we don't seem to have an ongoing API method tracker. The following (web platform) test shows this problem:

```html
<!doctype html>
<script src="/resources/testharness.js"></script>
<script src="/resources/testharnessreport.js"></script>
<a id="link" href="#link-target"></a>
<body>
<script>
promise_test(async t => {
  let start_length = navigation.entries().length;
  let navState = { baz: "qux" };

  navigation.onnavigate = t.step_func(e => {
    e.intercept({
      precommitHandler: t.step_func(controller => {
        // this should always go kablooey, given that ongoing API method tracker is null and we're trying to use it in step 10.3
        controller.redirect("#redirected2", { state: navState });
      }),
    });
  });

  // could use link click here for the same result, but then `navigation.transition.finished`
  // is undefined in gecko
  location.hash = "#hash-target";
  await navigation.transition.finished;

  assert_equals(location.hash, "#redirected2");
  // blink fails this assertion, gecko has already crashed in the redirect
  assert_equals(navigation.currentEntry.getState().baz, "qux");
}, "precommitHandler redirect() with state on non-API navigation (location.hash)");
</script>
</body>
```

Suggested fix I think could be simple, make 10.3 conditional on if we have an ongoing API method tracker or not. But should we not warn the user that they're using the API in ways that it does not support? Because clearly, blink is not able to serialize the state (the assertion fails)

Bugzilla bug that tripped up this spec bug: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2068197
